<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lester B. Pearson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lester Pearson, Lester Bowles Pearson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lester B. Pearson was a Canadian diplomat and Liberal prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> from 1963 to 1968. As foreign minister he proposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-emergency-force">United Nations Emergency Force</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> of 1956, for which he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1957. His governments introduced the Canada Pension Plan, national medicare and the Maple Leaf flag.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lester-b-pearson.jpg" alt="Lester B. Pearson" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ABC Television · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lester_B._Pearson_(1963_ABC_press_photo).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
